WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:05.430
SPEAKER: Welcome
to the quickstart

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.550
for Cloud Speech-to-Text,
which converts speech into text

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.830
using Google's AI technologies.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.510
In this video, we
walk through how

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.360
to convert an audio file to
text using the REST interface

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.550
and the cURL command.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.470
To get started with
Cloud Speech-to-Text,

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.430
you need a Google Cloud project.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.460
You can use an existing
project or set up

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.590
a new one in the
Google Cloud Console

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.535
at console.cloud.google.com.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.880
Visit the APIs &amp; Services
section of the Console

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.560
and enable the
Cloud Speech-to-Text

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.565
API for your project.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.240
Next, you need to set
up authentication.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.780
In the APIs &amp; Services
section, click on Credentials.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.780
Create a new service account
and download the private key

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.900
as JSON.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.170
You can use these credentials
to access Google Cloud APIs.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.900
So make sure to keep this file
secure and out of your code

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.690
and public repositories.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.510
To securely access the
credentials from your project,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.760
go to your terminal and
set an environment variable

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.817
to the path of your JSON file.

00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:10.400
If you don't yet
have the Google Cloud

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.450
SDK installed on your machine,
then install and initialize it.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.250
Instructions are linked below.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.850
Create a new JSON request
file, add the following text,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.120
and save it as a
sync-request.json plaintext

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.590
file.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.900
This JSON snippet indicates
that the audio file

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.950
has a FLAC encoding format, a
sample rate of 16,000 hertz,

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.810
and is stored in a
Google Cloud Storage

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.670
bucket at the given URI.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.050
Next, use cURL to make a request
to the speech.googleapis.com

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.370
endpoint.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.240
This cURL command specifies
sync-request.json as the file

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.410
name of the JSON request
you just created.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.970
Run the cURL command,
and you should

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.740
see the following response.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.650
Here, we can see the transcribed
text as well as a confidence

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.560
value that estimates how likely
it is that the transcription is

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.540
accurate.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.750
And that's it.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.290
You now know how to make a call
to the Cloud Speech-to-Text

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.362
API using a cURL command.

00:02:06.362 --> 00:02:08.820
Make sure to check out the rest
of our Cloud Speech-to-Text

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.870
documentation at
cloud.google.com/speech

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.510
to text/docs.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

